# Generated release artifacts; retain locally or upload as CI evidence
release/bundle/
release/evidence/
# The audit and the SBOM are **generated** by the build, and the build
# rewrites them with a fresh date. Tracking them meant the build dirtied
# the tree it was building from, and the candidate's "dirty" flag said
# true on every run — which made the flag meaningless. They are still
# part of the candidate, and the CI still uploads them as evidence.
release/dependency-audit.json
release/sbom.json
# The acceptance evidence is **generated** by the gates — the pilot, the
# isolated install and the live test — and every one of them rewrites its
# file with a fresh date. Tracking them meant that running a gate dirtied
# the tree it had just verified, and the candidate's "dirty" flag said
# true on every run. The gates are what produce them, and the CI uploads
# them as evidence; the repository does not need to carry them.
fixtures/acceptance/release/pilot.json
fixtures/acceptance/release/isolated-install.json
fixtures/acceptance/release/live-artifact.json
# The candidate manifest is regenerated for every build, and it names the
# revision it was built from. Committing it would make the tree dirty the
# moment it is regenerated, so it would never agree with the commit it
# describes. The authoritative copy is the one the CI uploads.
release/candidate.json
